NNAAMMEE
       sprof - read and display shared object profiling data

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       sspprrooff [_o_p_t_i_o_n ...] _s_h_a_r_e_d_‐_o_b_j_e_c_t_‐_p_a_t_h [_p_r_o_f_i_l_e_‐_d_a_t_a_‐_p_a_t_h]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  sspprrooff  command  displays a profiling summary for the shared object
       (shared library) specified as its  first  command‐line  argument.   The
       profiling  summary is created using previously generated profiling data
       in the (optional) second command‐line argument.  If the profiling  data
       pathname  is  omitted,  then  sspprrooff will attempt to deduce it using the
       soname of the shared object, looking for a file  with  the  name  _<_s_o_n_‐
       _a_m_e_>_._p_r_o_f_i_l_e in the current directory.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       The  following  command‐line  options  specify the profile output to be
       produced:

       ----ccaallll--ppaaiirrss
       --cc     Print a list of pairs of call paths for the interfaces  exported
              by  the  shared object, along with the number of times each path
              is used.

       ----ffllaatt--pprrooffiillee
       --pp     Generate a flat profile of all of the functions in the monitored
              object, with counts and ticks.

       ----ggrraapphh
       --qq     Generate a call graph.

       If none of the above options is specified, then the default behavior is
       to display a flat profile and a call graph.

       The following additional command‐line options are available:

       ----hheellpp
       --??     Display a summary of  command‐line  options  and  arguments  and
              exit.

       ----uussaaggee
              Display a short usage message and exit.

       ----vveerrssiioonn
       --VV     Display the program version and exit.

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
       GNU.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       The  following example demonstrates the use of sspprrooff.  The example con‐
       sists of a main program that calls two functions in  a  shared  object.
       First, the code of the main program:

           $ ccaatt pprroogg..cc;
           #include <stdlib.h>

           void x1(void);
           void x2(void);

           int
           main(int argc, char *argv[])
           {
               x1();
               x2();
               exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
           }

       The  functions  _x_1()  and _x_2() are defined in the following source file
       that is used to construct the shared object:

           $ ccaatt lliibbddeemmoo..cc;
           #include <unistd.h>

           void
           consumeCpu1(int lim)
           {
               for (unsigned int j = 0; j < lim; j++)
                getppid();
           }

           void
           x1(void) {
               for (unsigned int j = 0; j < 100; j++)
                consumeCpu1(200000);
           }

           void
           consumeCpu2(int lim)
           {
               for (unsigned int j = 0; j < lim; j++)
                getppid();
           }

           void
           x2(void)
           {
               for (unsigned int j = 0; j < 1000; j++)
                consumeCpu2(10000);
           }

       Now we construct the shared object with the real name _l_i_b_d_e_m_o_._s_o_._1_._0_._1,
       and the soname _l_i_b_d_e_m_o_._s_o_._1:

           $ cccc --gg --ffPPIICC --sshhaarreedd --WWll,,--ssoonnaammee,,lliibbddeemmoo..ssoo..11 \\
                   --oo lliibbddeemmoo..ssoo..11..00..11 lliibbddeemmoo..cc;

       Then we construct symbolic links for the library soname and the library
       linker name:

           $ llnn --ssff lliibbddeemmoo..ssoo..11..00..11 lliibbddeemmoo..ssoo..11;
           $ llnn --ssff lliibbddeemmoo..ssoo..11 lliibbddeemmoo..ssoo;

       Next, we compile the main program, linking it against  the  shared  ob‐
       ject, and then list the dynamic dependencies of the program:

           $ cccc --gg --oo pprroogg pprroogg..cc --LL.. --llddeemmoo;
           $ lldddd pprroogg;
                linux-vdso.so.1 =>  (0x00007fff86d66000)
                libdemo.so.1 => not found
                libc.so.6 => /lib64/libc.so.6 (0x00007fd4dc138000)
                /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2 (0x00007fd4dc51f000)

       In  order to get profiling information for the shared object, we define
       the environment variable LLDD__PPRROOFFIILLEE with the soname of the library:

           $ eexxppoorrtt LLDD__PPRROOFFIILLEE==lliibbddeemmoo..ssoo..11;

       We then define the  environment  variable  LLDD__PPRROOFFIILLEE__OOUUTTPPUUTT  with  the
       pathname  of  the directory where profile output should be written, and
       create that directory if it does not exist already:

           $ eexxppoorrtt LLDD__PPRROOFFIILLEE__OOUUTTPPUUTT==$$((ppwwdd))//pprrooff__ddaattaa;
           $ mmkkddiirr --pp $$LLDD__PPRROOFFIILLEE__OOUUTTPPUUTT;

       LLDD__PPRROOFFIILLEE causes profiling output to be _a_p_p_e_n_d_e_d to the output file if
       it already exists, so we ensure that there is no preexisting  profiling
       data:

           $ rrmm --ff $$LLDD__PPRROOFFIILLEE__OOUUTTPPUUTT//$$LLDD__PPRROOFFIILLEE..pprrooffiillee;

       We then run the program to produce the profiling output, which is writ‐
       ten to a file in the directory specified in LLDD__PPRROOFFIILLEE__OOUUTTPPUUTT:

           $ LLDD__LLIIBBRRAARRYY__PPAATTHH==.. ..//pprroogg;
           $ llss pprrooff__ddaattaa;
           libdemo.so.1.profile

       We  then use the sspprrooff --pp option to generate a flat profile with counts
       and ticks:

           $ sspprrooff --pp lliibbddeemmoo..ssoo..11 $$LLDD__PPRROOFFIILLEE__OOUUTTPPUUTT//lliibbddeemmoo..ssoo..11..pprrooffiillee;
           Flat profile:

           Each sample counts as 0.01 seconds.
             %   cumulative   self              self     total
            time   seconds   seconds    calls  us/call  us/call  name
            60.00      0.06     0.06      100   600.00           consumeCpu1
            40.00      0.10     0.04     1000    40.00           consumeCpu2
             0.00      0.10     0.00        1     0.00           x1
             0.00      0.10     0.00        1     0.00           x2

       The sspprrooff --qq option generates a call graph:

           $ sspprrooff --qq lliibbddeemmoo..ssoo..11 $$LLDD__PPRROOFFIILLEE__OOUUTTPPUUTT//lliibbddeemmoo..ssoo..11..pprrooffiillee;

           index % time    self  children    called     name

                           0.00    0.00      100/100         x1 [1]
           [0]    100.0    0.00    0.00      100         consumeCpu1 [0]
           -----------------------------------------------
                           0.00    0.00        1/1           <UNKNOWN>
           [1]      0.0    0.00    0.00        1         x1 [1]
                           0.00    0.00      100/100         consumeCpu1 [0]
           -----------------------------------------------
                           0.00    0.00     1000/1000        x2 [3]
           [2]      0.0    0.00    0.00     1000         consumeCpu2 [2]
           -----------------------------------------------
                           0.00    0.00        1/1           <UNKNOWN>
           [3]      0.0    0.00    0.00        1         x2 [3]
                           0.00    0.00     1000/1000        consumeCpu2 [2]
           -----------------------------------------------

       Above and below, the "<UNKNOWN>" strings represent identifiers that are
       outside of the profiled object (in this example, these are instances of
       _m_a_i_n_(_)).

       The sspprrooff --cc option generates a list of call pairs and  the  number  of
       their occurrences:

           $ sspprrooff --cc lliibbddeemmoo..ssoo..11 $$LLDD__PPRROOFFIILLEE__OOUUTTPPUUTT//lliibbddeemmoo..ssoo..11..pprrooffiillee;
           <UNKNOWN>                  x1                                 1
           x1                         consumeCpu1                      100
           <UNKNOWN>                  x2                                 1
           x2                         consumeCpu2                     1000

